//      scalar.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: empty-string sentinel rejected.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one unpacked-dimensions state per
//      declarator.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: heterogeneous payloads.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog unpacked-dimensions-presence
// classifier.
type UnpackedDimensionsClause
  = NoUnpackedDimensions
  | UnpackedDimensionsLexeme { lexeme: String }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VariableInitializerClause — the closed Verilog variable-declarator
// initializer-presence classifier (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.4). Optional
// per spec — present-or-absent partition.
//
//   - NoVariableInitializer
//       — `reg a;` form: no initializer.
//   - VariableInitializerLexeme { lexeme: String }
//       — `reg a = 8'h00;` form: initializer as a verbatim lexeme.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Two-way present/absent partition.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted: same shape
// as UnpackedDimensionsClause; abbreviated for brevity (each pattern's
// argument is identical modulo "unpacked-dimensions" vs "variable-
// initializer"). The pre-#3176-openai-pro shape pointed at
// NetInitializerClause, which has since been dissolved structurally
// by NetDeclaration's per-form Disj split.
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: shared classifier across emitters /
//      simulation-init / elaboration consumers.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: empty-string sentinel rejected.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one initializer state per declarator.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: heterogeneous payloads.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog variable-initializer-
// presence classifier.
type VariableInitializerClause
  = NoVariableInitializer
  | VariableInitializerLexeme { lexeme: String }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NetDeclaration — a Verilog net declaration (`wire foo;`,
// `wire [7:0] bus;`, `wire signed [15:0] s;`, `wand x;`,
// `wire (strong0, weak1) #5 a = expr_a, b = expr_b;`, `trireg
// (small) [3:0] cap_bus;`, `trireg (weak0, weak1) #2 stor = expr_s;`).
// IEEE 1364-2005 §3.10 / §A.2.1.3.
//
// Per spec §A.2.1.3 net_declaration is a FOUR-WAY closed alternative
// set, NOT a free product of {net_type, strength, form, declarators}:
//
//   net_declaration ::=
//     net_type [drive_strength] [vectored|scalared] [signed] [range]
//       [delay3] list_of_net_decl_assignments ;
//   | net_type [vectored|scalared] [signed] [range] [delay3]
//       list_of_net_identifiers ;
//   | trireg [charge_strength] [vectored|scalared] [signed] [range]
//       [delay3] list_of_net_identifiers ;
//   | trireg [drive_strength] [vectored|scalared] [signed] [range]
//       [delay3] list_of_net_decl_assignments ;
//
// where `net_type` (per §3.7.1) is one of the eleven non-`trireg`
// keywords; `trireg` is the fourth-row net-type with distinct
// strength-and-form admissibility.
//
// Modeling the four spec alternatives as STRUCTURAL variants makes
// these illegal states unrepresentable (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES
// on PR #3176 2026-05-16; same INVARIANTS P2 / Practice-2 discipline
// as VariableDeclaration's kind×packing structural split below):
//
//   - drive_strength on the identifier-only form  (forbidden by spec)
//   - charge_strength on a non-trireg net_type    (forbidden by spec)
//   - mixed init/non-init declarator lists         (forbidden by spec)
//   - identifier-only declarators with initializers (forbidden by spec)
//   - assignment-form declarators without initializers (forbidden)
//
// The discriminating axes are STRUCTURAL per variant: {trireg?} ×
// {form: identifier-only vs assignment}. The non-trireg-net-type
// keyword set (`wire / tri / wand / triand / wor / trior / tri0 /
// tri1 / supply0 / supply1 / uwire`) rides as a `NonTriregNetKind`
// field on the two non-trireg variants; the trireg variants do NOT
// carry a `net_type` field — the variant identity IS the net_type
// fact. `drive_strength` and `charge_strength` PRESENCE remain inside
// their respective Clause Disjs (DriveStrengthClause /
// ChargeStrengthClause), not as a top-level variant axis — strength
// is optional per spec brackets, and the existing Clause Disjs
// already structurally encode present-vs-absent.
//
//   - NonTriregIdentifierForm — `wire [7:0] a, b, c;` (no drive_
//                               strength permitted on this spec
//                               alternative; declarators are
//                               name-only).
//   - NonTriregAssignForm     — `wire (strong0, weak1) [7:0] a =
//                               e1, b = e2;` (optional drive_
//                               strength; declarators carry
//                               required initializers).
//   - TriregIdentifierForm    — `trireg (small) [3:0] cap_bus;`
//                               (optional charge_strength;
//                               declarators are name-only).
//   - TriregAssignForm        — `trireg (weak0, weak1) #2 stor =
//                               e_s;` (optional drive_strength;
//                               declarators carry required
//                               initializers).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every net-declaration emitter +
//      every elaboration / well-formedness consumer reads the
//      net_declaration uniformly; not a scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing into `{ is_trireg: Bool,
//      has_assignments: Bool, drive_strength, charge_strength, … }`
//      reintroduces the admit-then-check shape this Disj exists to
//      dissolve (non-trireg + charge_strength becomes representable;
//      identifier-form + drive_strength becomes representable).
//      Structural Disj forecloses.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the {trireg?} × {form}
//      partition is the spec's §A.2.1.3 irreducible structural axis,
//      not a richer std/ algebraic source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a net_declaration is EXACTLY ONE of
//      the four spec forms; mutually exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four variants are
//      structurally heterogeneous (kind-vs-no-kind, drive-vs-charge-
//      vs-neither strength, assignment-vs-identifier declarators) —
//      no generic `F<X>` projection over a richer source set
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed four-way IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.1.3
// net_declaration alternative set.
//
// Prior shape (single Conj-record with free {net_type, drive_strength,
// declarators: List<NetDeclarator>}) was the codex BLOCKING-fix shape
// on PR #3176 2026-05-16; openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES at sha 6f587e6c
// correctly flagged that it admitted the five spec-illegal states
// above as a free product. This four-variant structural split is
// the dissolution.
type NetDeclaration
  = NonTriregIdentifierForm {
      kind: NonTriregNetKind
      net_packing: NetVectoredPacking
      signedness: Signedness
      delay: DelayClause
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.1.3 list_of_net_identifiers) —
      // deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note above.
      identifiers: List<NetIdentifierDeclarator>
    }
  | NonTriregAssignForm {
      kind: NonTriregNetKind
      drive_strength: DriveStrengthClause
      net_packing: NetVectoredPacking
      signedness: Signedness
      delay: DelayClause
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.1.3 list_of_net_decl_assignments) —
      // deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note above.
      assignments: List<NetAssignmentDeclarator>
    }
  | TriregIdentifierForm {
      charge_strength: ChargeStrengthClause
      net_packing: NetVectoredPacking
      signedness: Signedness
      delay: DelayClause
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.1.3 list_of_net_identifiers) —
      // deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note above.
      identifiers: List<NetIdentifierDeclarator>
    }
  | TriregAssignForm {
      drive_strength: DriveStrengthClause
      net_packing: NetVectoredPacking
      signedness: Signedness
      delay: DelayClause
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.1.3 list_of_net_decl_assignments) —
      // deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note above.
      assignments: List<NetAssignmentDeclarator>
    }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NetAssignmentDeclarator — one (net_identifier, initializer-
// expression) pair in a net_declaration's list_of_net_decl_assignments
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.4: `net_decl_assignment ::= net_identifier =
// expression`). A single `wire [7:0] a = e_a, b = e_b;` carries TWO
// NetAssignmentDeclarators. The initializer is REQUIRED on every
// element of the assignment-form (no `[]` brackets in the spec
// production) — it is structurally non-optional here, dissolving the
// prior optional-initializer Disj on a unified NetDeclarator type
// (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 2026-05-16). Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name             the declared net identifier (K-1-opaque
//                      Symbol).
//   - rhs_lexeme       the initializer expression as a verbatim
//                      lexeme. Sub-grammar deferred to the bundled
//                      T-4 LanguageModel via the established
//                      body_lexeme precedent.
type NetAssignmentDeclarator {
  name: Symbol
  rhs_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NetIdentifierDeclarator — one element of a net_declaration's
// list_of_net_identifiers (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.3 / §A.2.1.3
// identifier-only form). Carries the net identifier and optional
// unpacked-array dimensions: the identifier-only spec form forbids
// initializers structurally (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176
// 2026-05-16 — making "identifier-only-with-initializer"
// unrepresentable rather than a runtime check). Per §A.2.3:
// `net_identifier [ dimension { dimension } ]` — each identifier
// admits an OPTIONAL unpacked-array dimension suffix
// (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 sha 4ccc6267 added this
// field; the prior shape dropped the per-identifier dimensions fact).
// A single `wire [7:0] a, b[0:1023], c;` carries THREE
// NetIdentifierDeclarators (the second with unpacked dimensions).
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name                 the declared net identifier (K-1-opaque
//                          Symbol).
//   - unpacked_dimensions  optional unpacked-array dimensions per
//                          §A.2.3 (reuses UnpackedDimensionsClause).
type NetIdentifierDeclarator {
  name: Symbol
  unpacked_dimensions: UnpackedDimensionsClause
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VariableDeclaration — a Verilog variable (`reg foo;`, `reg [31:0]
// counter;`, `reg signed [15:0] s;`, `integer i;`, `real x;`,
// `time t;`, `realtime rt;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §3.7.2, §5.7 partitions
// variable declarations into five kinds, where ONLY `reg` admits a
// `[msb:lsb]` packing — the other four (`integer` / `real` / `time`
// / `realtime`) have spec-fixed widths and forbid a vector range
// (`[N:M] integer i;` is a syntax error per §5.7). The kind × packing
// admissibility is modeled STRUCTURALLY: only `RegVariable` carries
// a `packing` payload, so "Integer with Packed range" is
// unrepresentable — not "rejected by a check" — the INVARIANTS P2
// illegal-states-unrepresentable shape (same design choice as
// node.dag's EdgeLabel, rust.dag's RustScalar Disj-with-named-
// payloads, and go.dag's GoScalar). The prior `{ var_type:
// VariableType, packing: PackingShape }` Conj-record-with-
// independent-coordinates form was the codex BLOCKING-review anti-
// pattern on PR #3176; this Disj-with-payloads form is the
// dissolution.
//
// The kind × signedness admissibility is also modeled STRUCTURALLY
// per IEEE 1364-2005 §5.6: `reg` admits `signed` (`reg signed [N:0]`
// is two's-complement signed bit-vector); `integer` is ALWAYS signed
// by spec (§3.7.2: "32-bit signed"); `time` is ALWAYS unsigned (the
// 64-bit simulation-time carrier); `real` and `realtime` are IEEE-754
// floating-point and carry NO signedness keyword (the floating-point
// sign is internal to the IEEE-754 representation, not a separate
// declaration modifier). Only `RegVariable` carries the
// `signedness: Signedness` payload — "RealVariable signedness=Signed"
// is unrepresentable, just like the kind × packing partition (codex
// BLOCKING fix 2026-05-16 on PR #3176; the spec-fixed-signedness
// kinds DO NOT need the carrier because the fact is non-variant per
// spec).
//
//   - RegVariable        — `reg [msb:lsb] name;` / `reg signed [msb:
//                          lsb] name;` / `reg name;` general-purpose
//                          variable; the procedural-assignment
//                          destination; THE ONLY variable-kind that
//                          admits packing AND signedness modifiers
//   - IntegerVariable    — `integer name;` 32-bit signed (spec-fixed
//                          signed); no packing, no signedness payload
//   - RealVariable       — `real name;` double-precision IEEE-754
//                          floating-point; no packing, no signedness
//                          payload (sign is internal to the IEEE-754
//                          representation, not a declaration modifier)
//   - TimeVariable       — `time name;` 64-bit unsigned simulation-
//                          time carrier (spec-fixed unsigned); no
//                          packing, no signedness payload
//   - RealTimeVariable   — `realtime name;` aliased to `real` per
//                          §3.7.2; no packing, no signedness payload
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A new variable-kind is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (SystemVerilog adds
// `logic` / `bit` / typed enums — a future language-version model
// would EDIT this Disj explicitly).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every variable-declaration emitter,
//      every procedural-assignment legality check (only RegVariable
//      is a legal procedural-assignment destination), every signed-
//      vs-unsigned arithmetic dispatch, and the per-kind algebra
//      inhabitance walk all read the variant tag uniformly; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{ kind: VariableType,
//      packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }` ratifies
//      multiple illegal states the spec forbids — "Integer with
//      Packed range" (§5.7) AND "Real with signedness=Signed" (§5.6
//      — real is floating-point, no signedness modifier), AND
//      "Time with signedness=Signed" (spec-fixed unsigned). The
//      Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads dissolves all of these:
//      only RegVariable carries packing AND signedness; the spec-
//      fixed-attribute kinds carry only `name`. Same dissolution
//      shape as rust.dag's RustScalar and go.dag's GoScalar.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (RegVariable → `Semiring<UInt<N>>` or
//      `OrderedRing<Int<N>>` per declared packing × signedness;
//      IntegerVariable → `OrderedRing<Int<32>>`; RealVariable /
//      RealTimeVariable → `ApproximateField<Float>`; TimeVariable
//      → `Semiring<UInt<64>>`). That grounding is INHABITANCE —
//      owned by the inhabiting carrier file (std/integer.dag,
//      std/float.dag) per P2, and DEFERRED to the operator-pending
//      D2 STOP. The variant set itself stays a closed classifier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single variable declaration IS
//      EXACTLY ONE variant; mutually-exclusive alternatives. The
//      shapes differ across variants (RegVariable carries packing
//      AND signedness; the rest carry neither) — they cannot be
//      coordinates of a single record without the illegal-state
//      smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five are structurally
//      heterogeneous (RegVariable carries packing + signedness; the
//      rest carry neither; the algebraic groundings are five
//      distinct std/algebra structures — Semiring or OrderedRing
//      depending on packing×signedness / OrderedRing<Int<32>> /
//      ApproximateField / Semiring<UInt<64>> / aliased
//      ApproximateField) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed five-variant Verilog variable-declaration
// classifier; illegal kind×packing AND kind×signedness combinations
// are unrepresentable by construction.
type VariableDeclaration
  // declarators: 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.4 / §7.4) — deferred to
  // std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where non_empty` refinement;
  // see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note above.
  = RegVariable { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness, declarators: List<VariableDeclarator> }
  | IntegerVariable { declarators: List<VariableDeclarator> }
  | RealVariable { declarators: List<VariableDeclarator> }
  | TimeVariable { declarators: List<VariableDeclarator> }
  | RealTimeVariable { declarators: List<VariableDeclarator> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// VariableDeclarator — one entry in a variable declaration's
// list_of_variable_decl_assignments (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.4 / §3.10).
// Per spec grammar `variable_type` is either `variable_identifier
// { dimension }` (named-with-optional-unpacked-dimensions) OR
// `variable_identifier = constant_expression` (initialized) —
// MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE alternatives, not independent coordinates. The
// prior {name, unpacked_dimensions, initializer} product shape
// admitted the spec-illegal `reg mem[0:1023] = 8'h00` combined
// dimensions+initializer state (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on
// PR #3176 sha 4ccc6267 — VariableDeclarator product-shape admits
// illegal states). Split into a closed Disj over the two §A.2.4
// alternatives makes the spec-illegal combined state structurally
// unrepresentable (INVARIANTS P2).
//
// A single declaration like `reg [7:0] a, mem[0:1023], c = 4'hF;`
// carries THREE VariableDeclarators (one NamedVariable per the first
// two names; one InitializedVariable for the last). The §7.4 PACKED
// dimensions stay on RegVariable's shared `packing` field at the
// outer level — those are TYPE-level, not declarator-level.
//
//   - NamedVariable
//       — `reg a;` / `reg mem[0:1023];` form: identifier with
//       OPTIONAL unpacked-array dimensions; NO initializer.
//   - InitializedVariable
//       — `reg c = 4'hF;` form: identifier with REQUIRED initializer
//       expression; NO unpacked-array dimensions.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.4 admits exactly the
// two variable_type alternatives.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every variable-declaration emitter
//      and elaboration consumer reads the per-declarator form
//      uniformly; shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ unpacked_dimensions,
//      initializer }` product (prior shape) admits the §A.2.4
//      spec-illegal combined dimensions+initializer state — exactly
//      the P2 illegal-states-representable concern this Disj
//      dissolves.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two alternatives
//      are the spec's §A.2.4 irreducible partition.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single variable_decl_assignment is
//      EXACTLY ONE form; mutually exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: NamedVariable carries
//      `unpacked_dimensions: UnpackedDimensionsClause`;
//      InitializedVariable carries `initializer:
//      VariableInitializerClause` — structurally heterogeneous
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog variable_decl_assignment
// alternative classifier of the IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.4 spec.
type VariableDeclarator
  = NamedVariable {
      name: Symbol
      unpacked_dimensions: UnpackedDimensionsClause
    }
  | InitializedVariable {
      name: Symbol
      initializer_lexeme: String
    }


// (PortDeclarationStyle has been dissolved into ModulePortsList's
// Disj-with-named-payloads at the MODULE level — IEEE 1364-2005
// §12.3.4 forbids mixing ANSI and non-ANSI styles within ONE module,
// so the style-choice belongs on the module's port-list as a whole,
// not as a per-port field. The prior per-port `style: PortDeclaration
// Style` admitted the spec-forbidden mixed-style state (codex BLOCKING
// review on PR #3176 surfaced this — the per-port encoding was a
// product-axis miscarve relative to the spec's module-header
// partition). See ModulePortsList below.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (PortAnsiTypeKind was DELETED on PR #3176 2026-05-16 per codex
// non-blocking improvement. The earlier generic-set carrier became
// orphaned once AnsiPortDeclaration adopted direction-specific
// payload types — input/inout use AnsiNetPortType, output uses
// OutputPortAnsiTypeKind. Keeping the unused 6-way generic carrier
// risked a parallel-authority drift between two carriers modeling
// the same ANSI-port type/kind space. The direction-specific
// classifiers below are the SOLE authority; future ANSI-port
// type/kind extensions (SystemVerilog `logic`, etc.) edit the
// direction-specific classifiers directly.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OutputPortAnsiTypeKind — the closed Verilog ANSI-OUTPUT-port
// type/kind classifier. IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3: "An output port can
// be of any net or variable type EXCEPT a real type" — floating-
// point types are not admitted as output ports because they have no
// well-defined inter-module wire representation. The output-specific
// admissible four-way partition is modeled here; the spec-illegal
// real / realtime output variants are unrepresentable by construction
// (codex BLOCKING fix on PR #3176 2026-05-16 — the prior shape
// reused a generic-output-port carrier admitting `output real` /
// `output realtime` which the spec forbids).
//
//   - OutputAnsiNetType { net_type: NetType, packing: PackingShape,
//                         signedness: Signedness }
//       — `output wire [7:0] data`; net family.
//   - OutputAnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
//       — `output reg [7:0] count`; reg-typed (procedural-assignable
//       output, the most common parameterized output form).
//   - OutputAnsiIntegerType
//       — `output integer counter`; 32-bit signed.
//   - OutputAnsiTimeType
//       — `output time t`; 64-bit unsigned simulation-time.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly
// this four-way output-type partition; SystemVerilog adds further
// types — future language-version model EDITS this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every output-port consumer reads the
//      kind to dispatch emission / type-check legality; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reusing a generic 6-variant output-
//      port carrier (the prior shape, predecessor to the direction-
//      specific split) admits `output real` / `output realtime` —
//      exactly the §12.3.3 illegal state this Disj dissolves. P2
//      illegal-states-unrepresentable.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (OutputAnsiNetType / OutputAnsiRegType →
//      Semiring or OrderedRing per packing × signedness;
//      OutputAnsiIntegerType → OrderedRing<Int<32>>;
//      OutputAnsiTimeType → Semiring<UInt<64>>). D2-deferred per
//      "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an ANSI output port has EXACTLY ONE
//      type/kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four variants are
//      structurally heterogeneous (OutputAnsiNetType carries net_type
//      + packing + signedness; OutputAnsiRegType carries packing +
//      signedness; OutputAnsiIntegerType + OutputAnsiTimeType are
//      nullary). NOT one generic `F<X>` projection over a richer
//      source set (Practice 7); the real/realtime exclusion is the
//      §12.3.3 spec's output-port-admissibility partition, not a
//      derived projection.
// Terminal: the closed five-way Verilog ANSI-output-port type/kind
// classifier (the no-net-no-reg variant added at sha 7b7dd0b54+
// per codex L1600 BLOCKING on PR #3176 — §A.1.3 output_declaration
// admits `output [net_type] [signed] [range]` AND `output [reg]
// [signed] [range]` where both `net_type` and `reg` are
// syntactically OPTIONAL; "output [signed] [range] name;" with
// neither keyword is a legal spec form that resolves to a net via
// §12.3.3 separate-declaration inheritance OR defaults to wire);
// real/realtime outputs unrepresentable by construction per IEEE
// 1364-2005 §12.3.3.
// (DELETED 2026-05-16 per openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176
// sha 98953d5f: this single 5-way Disj conflated two §A.1.3 spec-
// alternatives — net-form outputs (list_of_port_identifiers, no
// initializers) and variable-form outputs (list_of_variable_port_
// identifiers, with initializers). Replaced with two sibling Disjs
// OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind + OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind below,
// consumed by separate AnsiOutputNetPort / AnsiOutputVariablePort
// variants of AnsiPortDeclaration, structurally forbidding `output
// wire y = 1'b0` while admitting all spec-legal output forms.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind — the closed Verilog ANSI-output-port
// type/kind classifier for the NET form: `output [net_type] [signed]
// [range] list_of_port_identifiers` (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.1.3, first
// output_declaration alternative). The list of identifiers uses
// `list_of_port_identifiers` — plain port_identifier elements with
// NO per-port initializers. Per port_declaration spec the net_type
// keyword (when present) excludes `trireg` — this carrier therefore
// uses NonTriregNetKind directly, not the broader NetType. Real/
// realtime are not net types and are not admitted here.
//
//   - OutputAnsiNetType { kind: NonTriregNetKind, packing, signedness }
//       — `output wire [7:0] y`, `output wand a`, etc.; explicit
//       non-trireg net-type keyword.
//   - OutputAnsiNoNetType { packing, signedness }
//       — `output [7:0] y` (no net_type keyword); spec §A.1.3 makes
//       net_type bracketed/optional. Resolves to default wire OR
//       inherits from a separate later declaration per §12.3.3.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Two-way net-form output partition; a
// third would be an L-2 pin edit.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Five-pattern ledger abbreviated (same shape
// arguments as the prior OutputPortAnsiTypeKind ledger):
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: shared classifier across output-port
//      emitters/recognizers.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{kind:
//      Option<NonTriregNetKind>, packing, signedness}` re-admits the
//      illegal "trireg-output" + the "net-form-with-initializer"
//      states this Disj exists to dissolve.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: spec's §A.1.3
//      net-output partition.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: exactly one form per output port; mutually
//      exclusive.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: heterogeneous payloads
//      (OutputAnsiNetType carries `kind`; OutputAnsiNoNetType does
//      not). Practice 7.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Verilog ANSI-output-port NET-form
// type/kind classifier.
type OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind
  = OutputAnsiNetType { kind: NonTriregNetKind, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | OutputAnsiNoNetType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind — the closed Verilog ANSI-output-
// port type/kind classifier for the VARIABLE form: `output [reg]
// [signed] [range] list_of_variable_port_identifiers` /
// `output output_variable_type list_of_variable_port_identifiers`
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.1.3, second + third output_declaration
// alternatives). The list of identifiers uses
// `list_of_variable_port_identifiers` — each port_identifier admits
// an OPTIONAL initializer expression. Real/realtime are excluded
// per §12.3.3 ("An output port can be of any net or variable type
// EXCEPT a real type").
//
//   - OutputAnsiRegType { packing, signedness }
//       — `output reg [7:0] count`; spec-form 2.
//   - OutputAnsiIntegerType
//       — `output integer counter`; 32-bit signed (spec-form 3).
//   - OutputAnsiTimeType
//       — `output time t`; 64-bit unsigned simulation-time
//       (spec-form 3).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). Three-way variable-form output partition.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Five-pattern ledger abbreviated:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: shared output-port classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `{kind, packing?,
//      signedness?}` admits the §12.3.3 illegal `output real` /
//      `output realtime` + reg-fixed-width violations the per-variant
//      payload-presence rule dissolves.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in std/
//      algebra (Reg→Semiring/OrderedRing per packing×signedness;
//      Integer→OrderedRing<Int<32>>; Time→Semiring<UInt<64>>);
//      D2-deferred per Owned ELSEWHERE.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: exactly one form per output port.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: structurally heterogeneous
//      payloads + algebraic groundings (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed three-way Verilog ANSI-output-port VARIABLE-
// form type/kind classifier; real/realtime outputs unrepresentable
// by construction per §12.3.3.
type OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind
  = OutputAnsiRegType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | OutputAnsiIntegerType
  | OutputAnsiTimeType


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NonAnsiPort — a non-ANSI-style Verilog module-HEADER port entry
// (`module M (a, b, c);` — the header lists port-EXPRESSIONS only;
// direction lives in BODY port_declaration items per IEEE 1364-2005
// §12.3.2). Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - port_expression_lexeme   the port_expression as a verbatim
//                              lexeme (per spec grammar the
//                              port_expression can be a bare
//                              identifier `a`, a bit/part-select
//                              `a[3:0]`, a concatenation `{a, b[3:0]}`,
//                              or a renamed-port form
//                              `.external_name(internal_expression)`).
//                              Sub-grammar deferred to the T-4
//                              LanguageModel via the established
//                              body_lexeme precedent — at the
//                              vocabulary-slice layer the slice
//                              carries the port-expression text
//                              losslessly; downstream
//                              port-binding resolution is the
//                              LanguageModel grammar's concern.
//
// Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.2 the non-ANSI header carries port-
// expressions ONLY — port direction (input/output/inout) and any
// inline type/packing/signedness facts live in BODY
// `port_declaration` items (see PortDeclaration below + the
// PortDeclarationItem ModuleItem variant). The prior
// `PortDeclaration { direction, name }` on the header port-list
// collapsed this spec-boundary; corrected per codex BLOCKING on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16.
type NonAnsiPort {
  port_expression_lexeme: String
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PortDeclaration — a non-ANSI-style Verilog BODY port_declaration
// (`input a;`, `output b;`, `inout c;`). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 +
// §12.2 admit these as module-body items. Each declares port
// direction + name; the type/packing/signedness facts come from a
// MATCHING `NetDeclaration` / `VariableDeclaration` in the same
// module body (the non-ANSI separated form), OR — per spec — a body
// port_declaration may combine direction + type inline (`input wire
// [7:0] a;`). The combined form is structurally equivalent to ANSI
// and is admitted by the same `AnsiPortDeclaration` carrier surfaced
// as `BodyPortDeclarationItem` in ModuleItem; this PortDeclaration
// record covers the direction-only form.
//
//   - direction  input / output / inout
//   - name       the port identifier (K-1-opaque Symbol; resolves
//                through the namespace to the matching body
//                Net/Variable declaration that carries the type-
//                fact, when separated)
//
// Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.4 / §12.2 PortDeclaration is a MODULE
// BODY item (PortDeclarationItem in ModuleItem), NOT a module-
// header element. The non-ANSI header carries port-EXPRESSIONS via
// NonAnsiPort above; this body declaration carries the direction
// fact that the non-ANSI form requires. Corrected per codex
// BLOCKING on PR #3176 2026-05-16 — the prior shape mis-located
// PortDeclaration on the header port-list, conflating the spec's
// header-vs-body partition.
//
// Per spec §A.2.1.2 grammar a single direction-only port_declaration
// admits a NON-EMPTY list of port identifiers under one shared
// direction keyword (`input a, b, c;` is one statement declaring
// three ports, not three statements). The prior singular-name shape
// lost the §A.2.1.2 statement-boundary (shared direction keyword
// across multi-identifier forms) — openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16 at line 1429; same shape as the
// DefparamDeclaration / EventDeclaration / GenvarDeclaration list-
// form precedent.
//
//   - direction      the port-direction keyword (input | output |
//                    inout); shared across all declared names in
//                    this statement.
//   - names          the non-empty list of declared port identifiers
//                    (K-1-opaque Symbols).
type PortDeclaration {
  direction: PortDirection
  // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.2.1.2 list_of_port_identifiers) — deferred
  // to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where non_empty`
  // refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note below.
  names: List<Symbol>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiNetPortType — the closed type/payload variants admitted for an
// ANSI port that ONLY accepts net types (input + inout ports per IEEE
// 1364-2005 §12.3.3). Input and inout ports cannot be reg-typed
// (procedural assignment requires reg, which only output can carry),
// and cannot be the spec-fixed-width variable kinds (integer / real /
// time / realtime are output-only per spec). This carrier is the
// closed type-payload set for input / inout — strictly the net family.
//
//   - AnsiInputInoutNet { kind: NonTriregNetKind, packing: PackingShape,
//                         signedness: Signedness }
//       — any non-trireg net-type variant (wire/wand/wor/tri/etc.)
//       with optional packing + signedness per spec §5.6. Per
//       port_declaration §A.1.3 grammar, `trireg` is excluded at
//       port positions (it is a net-declaration-only form);
//       NonTriregNetKind enforces this structurally (openai-pro
//       REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 sha 98953d5f — the prior `NetType`
//       field admitted spec-illegal `input trireg p` / `output trireg p`).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly net
// types for input/inout ANSI ports; SystemVerilog `logic` would EDIT
// this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Single-variant Disj (currently exactly one
// type-shape — net with payload — admitted; future spec extensions
// would add variants). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every input/inout-ANSI consumer reads
//      the type uniformly to dispatch emission / drive net-resolution;
//      shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the single variant carries a
//      structured payload (net_type + packing + signedness); a
//      uniform record would collapse the future-extension axis a
//      Disj preserves.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: net types are the spec's
//      primitive resolution-function set per §3.7.1; D2-deferred per
//      "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an input/inout ANSI port has EXACTLY ONE
//      net-type payload; the variant is the dispatcher.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the single variant's payload is
//      a structured record (NetType × PackingShape × Signedness),
//      not one generic `F<X>` projection (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way net-type-presence-aware type-payload
// classifier for input / inout ANSI ports (the no-net-type variant
// added at sha 7b7dd0b54+ per codex L1600 BLOCKING on PR #3176 —
// §A.1.3 `input [net_type] [signed] [range]` makes net_type
// SYNTACTICALLY OPTIONAL; the prior single-variant shape forced
// every input/inout port to claim a net_type, dropping the spec's
// "port_declaration without type, type provided by a separate
// later net/reg declaration" §12.3.3 form).
type AnsiNetPortType
  = AnsiInputInoutNet { kind: NonTriregNetKind, packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | AnsiInputInoutNoNetType { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiPortDeclaration — an ANSI-style Verilog module port (`module M
// (input wire [7:0] a, output reg b, output reg signed [3:0] count,
// inout tri bus, …);`). The header carries direction, name, AND a
// direction-specific type payload. Per IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 the
// port declaration is "completely declared" by this single carrier;
// a body redeclaration is a SYNTAX ERROR.
//
// Direction × type-kind admissibility is modeled STRUCTURALLY via
// Disj-with-named-payloads per direction (codex BLOCKING fix on
// PR #3176 2026-05-16: the prior uniform `{ direction, name, type_
// kind }` Conj record admitted spec-forbidden combinations like
// `input reg [N:0]` and `inout integer` — INVARIANTS P2 illegal-
// states-unrepresentable violation. Per spec §12.3.3 input/inout
// ports MUST be net-typed; only output ports admit reg/integer/real/
// time/realtime variable kinds. The per-direction split makes those
// illegal combinations structurally unrepresentable, mirroring the
// VariableDeclaration kind×packing×signedness dissolution).
//
//   - AnsiInputPort { net: AnsiNetPortType, names: List<Symbol> }
//       — input ports are net-typed only (the spec forbids
//       `input reg`); names form a §A.1.3 list_of_port_identifiers.
//   - AnsiOutputNetPort { type_kind: OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind,
//                        names: List<Symbol> }
//       — output net-form (§A.1.3 output_declaration alternative 1:
//       `output [net_type] [signed] [range] list_of_port_identifiers`).
//       The 2-way OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind partition (explicit
//       non-trireg net-type / no-net-type) covers the spec alternatives;
//       names use plain port_identifiers — NO per-port initializers
//       structurally representable.
//   - AnsiOutputVariablePort { type_kind: OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind,
//                             names: List<AnsiOutputPortDeclarator> }
//       — output variable-form (§A.1.3 output_declaration alternatives
//       2+3: `output [reg] [signed] [range]` /
//       `output output_variable_type` followed by
//       list_of_variable_port_identifiers). The 3-way
//       OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind partition (reg / integer / time)
//       covers spec-admissible variable kinds; names use
//       list_of_variable_port_identifiers with optional per-port
//       initializers via AnsiOutputPortDeclarator. Real/realtime
//       outputs excluded per §12.3.3. The net-vs-variable AnsiOutputPort
//       split corrected the prior unified-declarator shape per openai-pro
//       REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3176 sha 98953d5f — the prior shape
//       admitted spec-illegal `output wire y = 1'b0` net-output-with-
//       initializer states.
//   - AnsiInoutPort { net: AnsiNetPortType, names: List<Symbol> }
//       — inout ports are net-typed only (bidirectional requires
//       nets; `inout reg` is a spec violation); names form a
//       §A.1.3 list_of_port_identifiers.
//
// All three variants gained an `attributes: AttributeInstanceClause`
// prefix and a non-empty `names` list at sha 4ccc6267 per openai-pro
// REQUEST_CHANGES (Layer-Model BLOCKING) — the prior single-name-no-
// attributes shape dropped §A.1.3 attribute-instance and
// list_of_port_identifiers declaration-boundary facts.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §12.3.3 admits exactly
// these three direction-bearing ANSI-port forms; SystemVerilog
// `ref` ports would EDIT this Disj explicitly.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ANSI-port consumer branches on
//      direction × type-kind to dispatch emission / type-check
//      legality / drive net-vs-reg-assignment-legality; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform
//      `{ direction: PortDirection, name: Symbol, type_payload:
//      <generic-ANSI-type-set> }` ratifies spec-forbidden
//      combinations (Input + reg-typed payload, Inout + integer-
//      typed payload, etc.) — the product-axis admits illegal states
//      this per-direction Disj dissolves. INVARIANTS P2 illegal-
//      states-unrepresentable.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three direction-
//      bearing ANSI forms are the spec's primitive direction × type
//      admissibility table — not a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single ANSI port is EXACTLY ONE
//      direction variant; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: AnsiInputPort and AnsiInoutPort
//      carry `net: AnsiNetPortType` (net-only, 2-variant: explicit
//      non-trireg-kind + no-net-type); AnsiOutputNetPort carries
//      `type_kind: OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind` (2-variant net forms)
//      + `names: List<Symbol>`; AnsiOutputVariablePort carries
//      `type_kind: OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind` (3-variant
//      variable forms: reg/integer/time) + `names: List<
//      AnsiOutputPortDeclarator>` (with optional initializers). The
//      payload types differ structurally — NOT one generic
//      `F<direction>` projection over a single source set
//      (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed four-way Verilog ANSI-port direction-kind
// classifier; illegal direction × type-kind combinations are
// unrepresentable by construction.
// As of sha 7b7dd0b54+ (codex L3563 BLOCKING on PR #3176): the
// `attributes: AttributeInstanceClause` field has been STRIPPED from
// all three variants below — AnsiPortDeclaration is now the
// attribute-FREE per-spec port declaration payload, reusable at:
//   - ANSI header position via AnsiHeaderPort wrapper (carries the
//     §A.1.3 outer `{attribute_instance}` prefix)
//   - non-ANSI body position via NonAnsiBodyPortDeclarationItem
//     (whose outer `attributes` field is the single authority)
// This dissolves the §A.1.3 double-authority that arose when both
// the outer wrapper AND the inner AnsiPortDeclaration carried
// attribute slots for one syntactic site (same shape as the
// OrderedPortConnection / NamedPortConnection split that resolved
// the analogous PortConnectionActual double-authority).
type AnsiPortDeclaration
  = AnsiInputPort {
      net: AnsiNetPortType
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.1.3 list_of_port_identifiers) — deferred
      // to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where non_empty`
      // refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note below.
      names: List<Symbol>
    }
  | AnsiOutputNetPort {
      // Spec §A.1.3 output_declaration form 1: `output [net_type]
      // [signed] [range] list_of_port_identifiers`. Net-form outputs
      // use plain port_identifiers — NO per-port initializers
      // structurally representable (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES on
      // PR #3176 sha 98953d5f: prior unified AnsiOutputPort admitted
      // `output wire y = 1'b0` which the spec forbids).
      type_kind: OutputPortAnsiNetTypeKind
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.1.3 list_of_port_identifiers) —
      // deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note below.
      names: List<Symbol>
    }
  | AnsiOutputVariablePort {
      // Spec §A.1.3 output_declaration forms 2+3: `output [reg]
      // [signed] [range] list_of_variable_port_identifiers` /
      // `output output_variable_type list_of_variable_port_
      // identifiers`. Variable-form outputs use
      // list_of_variable_port_identifiers — each port_identifier
      // admits an OPTIONAL initializer expression. Real/realtime
      // excluded per §12.3.3.
      type_kind: OutputPortAnsiVariableTypeKind
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.1.3 list_of_variable_port_identifiers)
      // — deferred to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where
      // non_empty` refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note below.
      names: List<AnsiOutputPortDeclarator>
    }
  | AnsiInoutPort {
      net: AnsiNetPortType
      // 🟡 spec-non-empty (§A.1.3 list_of_port_identifiers) — deferred
      // to std/collection.dag Wave-A2 `List<T> where non_empty`
      // refinement; see shared TRACKED-SCAFFOLD note below.
      names: List<Symbol>
    }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiHeaderPort — one ANSI-style module-header port entry (IEEE
// 1364-2005 §12.3.3 / §A.1.3). Conj wrapper providing the §A.1.3
// `{attribute_instance}` outer prefix around the attribute-free
// AnsiPortDeclaration payload. Sibling pattern of
// OrderedPortConnection / NamedPortConnection — outer-attribute
// placement matching the spec grammar's prefix position.
//
// Per §A.1.3 grammar:
//
//   port_declaration ::= {attribute_instance}
//                        (input_declaration | output_declaration
//                       | inout_declaration)
//
// the attributes live at the port_declaration level, ABOVE the
// direction-specific declaration sub-form. This wrapper provides
// that level for ANSI header use; the same AnsiPortDeclaration
// payload is reused at non-ANSI body position via
// NonAnsiBodyPortDeclarationItem (whose outer `attributes` field
// is the single authority for that position). Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - attributes   the optional {attribute_instance} prefix per
//                  §A.1.3 (reuses AttributeInstanceClause).
//   - decl         the direction-bearing port declaration payload
//                  (AnsiPortDeclaration). Attribute-free.
type AnsiHeaderPort {
  attributes: AttributeInstanceClause
  decl: AnsiPortDeclaration
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AnsiOutputPortDeclarator — one (name, optional-initializer) entry
// in an ANSI output port's list_of_variable_port_identifiers (IEEE
// 1364-2005 §A.1.3 / §A.2.3). Per spec list_of_variable_port_
// identifiers ::= port_identifier [ = constant_expression ] { ,
// port_identifier [ = constant_expression ] } — each output-port
// identifier admits an OPTIONAL initializer. Only ANSI output ports
// reach this carrier; input/inout ports use plain `List<Symbol>`
// because the spec form list_of_port_identifiers does NOT admit
// per-port initializers. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - name             the declared port identifier (K-1-opaque
//                      Symbol).
//   - initializer      the optional initializer clause (reuses
//                      VariableInitializerClause); structurally
//                      optional per the §A.2.3 brackets.
type AnsiOutputPortDeclarator {
  name: Symbol
  initializer: VariableInitializerClause
}


// (The standalone ModulePortsList Disj has been dissolved into
// VerilogModule's own per-style Disj — VerilogModule is now
// NonAnsiVerilogModule | AnsiVerilogModule, each variant carrying its
// own header-port list type AND its own admissible body-items list
// type. This lifts the port-style choice from the port-list carrier
// to the module-as-a-whole carrier, making "ANSI port-list module
// with body PortDeclarationItem" structurally unrepresentable —
// INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable fix per codex BLOCKING
// on PR #3176 2026-05-16. The §12.3.4 module-style partition is now
// the dimensional axis of VerilogModule itself; the rationale
// formerly housed here is folded into VerilogModule's ledger below.)


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ParameterTypeKind — the closed Verilog parameter-type classifier
// (IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.1.1). A parameter declaration's type is
// either UNTYPED (inferring from the default-value's type per §3.10)
// OR an explicit parameter_type (integer / real / realtime / time).
// The explicit-type form FORBIDS [signed]/[range] modifiers per
// spec — those apply only to the untyped form.
//
//   - UntypedParameter { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
//       — `parameter WIDTH = 8;`, `parameter signed [3:0] X = 4'sb1100;`
//       The packing + signedness modifiers apply ONLY to this form.
//   - IntegerParameter      — `parameter integer N = 4;`
//   - RealParameter         — `parameter real PI = 3.14;`
//   - TimeParameter         — `parameter time T = 10;`
//   - RealTimeParameter     — `parameter realtime RT = 1.5;`
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). IEEE 1364-2005 §A.2.1.1 admits exactly
// this five-way parameter-type partition.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every parameter consumer (override-
//      conversion per §12.1.3, type-check legality, emission)
//      reads the type kind uniformly; shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform `{ kind:
//      Symbol, packing?: PackingShape, signedness?: Signedness }`
//      ratifies spec-forbidden states like "IntegerParameter with
//      signed [3:0]" (per spec the explicit-type forms forbid
//      range/signedness modifiers). Disj-with-heterogeneous-
//      payloads makes the per-kind admissibility structural.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (UntypedParameter → polymorphic per the
//      default-value's inferred type; IntegerParameter →
//      OrderedRing<Int<32>>; RealParameter / RealTimeParameter →
//      ApproximateField<Float>; TimeParameter → Semiring<UInt<64>>).
//      D2-deferred per "Owned ELSEWHERE".
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single parameter has EXACTLY ONE
//      type kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: heterogeneous payload-presence
//      (UntypedParameter carries packing+signedness; the typed
//      kinds carry neither) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a single source set (Practice 7). Mirror of body-side
//      VariableDeclaration's kind × packing × signedness
//      dissolution.
// Terminal: the closed five-way Verilog parameter-type classifier.
type ParameterTypeKind
  = UntypedParameter { packing: PackingShape, signedness: Signedness }
  | IntegerParameter
  | RealParameter
  | TimeParameter
  | RealTimeParameter


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 🟡 TRACKED-SCAFFOLD: spec-non-empty list fields (relayed
// disposition from T-4 mgr vivid-carp-207 msg_30a7ec7c, 2026-05-16).
//
// Twenty-seven sites in this file carry `List<T>` payloads where IEEE
// 1364-2005 requires AT LEAST ONE entry — the spec-illegal empty
// state is structurally representable, the same INVARIANTS P2
// illegal-states-unrepresentable concern codex / openai-pro
// correctly flagged. The MOTIVATION is real and spec-grounded. The
// CANONICAL FIX is NOT a worker-minted `NonEmptyList<T>` generic
// carrier:
//
//   - `docs/coercion-design.md` RQ-3 (ratified): "refinements of
//     base containers, NOT separate inhabitant families.
//     `NonEmptyList<T> = List<T> where non_empty`. Separate
//     inhabitants would grow the authority surface." A hand-rolled
//     `NonEmptyList<T> { head: T, tail: List<T> }` IS a separate
//     inhabitant family and breaks the ratified Vec<T> coercion.
//   - `std/collection.dag` (merged on main) DEFERS its entire
//     finite-cardinality / refinement layer to "Wave-A2 — TRACKED
//     SCAFFOLD (🟡)". The `List<T> where non_empty` refinement IS
//     that deferred layer.
//   - This file's frozen scaffold header neither Owns nor Consumes
//     `std/collection.dag`. Minting a parallel non-empty carrier in
//     a leaf language probe would be a 5-language parallel-
//     declaration precedent — the v3 cross-layer spine-drift this
//     T-4 directive exists to prevent.
//
// SANCTIONED DEFERRAL (the deferral middle-path, mirroring
// collection.dag's own Wave-A2 scaffold + the ptx #3170 / spice #3168
// inhabitance-deferral pattern):
// - Each of the twenty-seven spec-non-empty sites keeps `List<T>` for now.
// - Each site carries a back-pointer comment to THIS shared note.
// - The empty-list illegal state is a PRODUCER-SIDE invariant
//   pending the refinement substrate landing.
// - DISSOLUTION TRIGGER: when `std/collection.dag` Wave-A2 lands the
//   non-empty refinement, the twenty-four sites adopt
//   `List<T> where non_empty` uniformly in one sweep — single-
//   authority preserved.
//
// The twenty-seven spec-non-empty sites (back-pointers to this note):
//   (sites 25-26 below — VerilogModule per-style header_ports)
//   1. NetDeclaration.NonTriregIdentifierForm.identifiers (§A.2.1.3)
//   2. NetDeclaration.NonTriregAssignForm.assignments     (§A.2.1.3)
//   3. NetDeclaration.TriregIdentifierForm.identifiers    (§A.2.1.3)
//   4. NetDeclaration.TriregAssignForm.assignments        (§A.2.1.3)
//   5. VariableDeclaration.{Reg,Integer,Real,Time,RealTime}Variable.declarators
//                                                    (§A.2.4 / §7.4)
//   6. ParameterDeclaration.assignments              (§A.2.1.1)
//   7. LocalParameterDeclaration.assignments         (§A.2.1.1)
//   8. SpecparamDeclaration.assignments              (§A.2.1.1)
//   9. ModuleParameters.parameters                   (§12.1)
//  10. AlwaysBlockSensitivity.ExplicitEventList.items (§9.7.2)
//  11. ParameterOverrides.OrderedParameterOverrides.actuals (§12.1.3)
//  12. ParameterOverrides.NamedParameterOverrides.overrides (§12.1.3)
//  13. ContinuousAssign.assignments                  (§6.1)
//  14. ModuleInstantiation.instances                 (§A.4.1.1)
//  15. PortConnections.PositionalConnections.actuals (§A.4.1.1)
